const page = { page: "lesson", paper: "paper-1", topic: "topic-2", unit: "2-2", lesson: "compilation-under-uthman" };
const title = "Compilation under &rsquo;Uthman &middot; 2.2 &middot; IGCSE Islamiyat";
const footerHtml = "Cambridge IGCSE Islamiyat 0493 &middot; Paper 1, Topic 2, Unit 2.2";
const mainHtml = "<section class=\"chapter\">\r\n      <div class=\"wrap\">\r\n        <div class=\"chapter-head\">\r\n          <h1>Compilation under &rsquo;Uthman</h1>\r\n          <p class=\"ch-sub\">2.2 The Compilation under the Khalifas &middot; c. 25 AH &middot; one standard text for a growing empire</p>\r\n        </div>\r\n\r\n        <p class=\"lede prose-col\">By the time of the third Caliph, &rsquo;Uthman ibn &rsquo;Affan, the Muslim empire had spread far beyond Arabia. That created a new problem &mdash; not a risk of losing the text, but a risk of the text <em>fragmenting</em>.</p>\r\n\r\n        <p class=\"prose-col\">The trigger came from a battlefield far from Madinah. The companion <strong>Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman</strong>, serving on campaigns in Armenia and Azerbaijan, watched soldiers from Iraq and Syria argue over whose recitation of the Qur&rsquo;an was correct &mdash; each side had learned authentic but differently-voiced oral traditions, and each was now accusing the other of getting it wrong. Alarmed, Hudhayfah hurried back to Madinah and warned &rsquo;Uthman directly:</p>\r\n\r\n        <div class=\"pull\">\r\n          <span class=\"translation\">&ldquo;Save this nation before they differ about the Book as the Jews and Christians differed about their Books before them.&rdquo;</span>\r\n          <span class=\"cite\">Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman, to Caliph &rsquo;Uthman ibn &rsquo;Affan</span>\r\n        </div>\r\n\r\n        <div class=\"steps\">\r\n          <div class=\"step\">\r\n            <span class=\"n\">1</span>\r\n            <div><h4>&rsquo;Uthman borrows Hafsa's mus-haf</h4><p>He sent for the original compilation kept by Hafsa bint &rsquo;Umar, to be used as the base copy &mdash; not started from scratch.</p></div>\r\n          </div>\r\n          <div class=\"step\">\r\n            <span class=\"n\">2</span>\r\n            <div><h4>A four-man committee, led again by Zayd ibn Thabit</h4><p>Alongside Zayd sat three Qurayshi companions: &rsquo;Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, Sa&rsquo;id ibn al-&rsquo;As, and &rsquo;Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham. Their task was not to simply copy &mdash; they independently re-verified the text against written material and living witnesses, exactly as Zayd had done under Abu Bakr.</p></div>\r\n          </div>\r\n          <div class=\"step\">\r\n            <span class=\"n\">3</span>\r\n            <div><h4>One rule for resolving disagreement</h4><p>&rsquo;Uthman instructed the three Qurayshi members: if they and Zayd (an Ansari, from Madinah) disagreed on how a word should be written, they were to write it in the dialect of Quraysh &mdash; &ldquo;for the Qur&rsquo;an was revealed in their tongue.&rdquo; This gave the project one clear, principled standard rather than an arbitrary compromise.</p></div>\r\n          </div>\r\n          <div class=\"step\">\r\n            <span class=\"n\">4</span>\r\n            <div><h4>Several copies, sent to the empire's centres</h4><p>Standard copies were produced and dispatched to the major garrison cities &mdash; Kufa, Basra, Damascus &mdash; and Makkah, with one retained in Madinah. Every Muslim community now had access to an identical, authoritative text.</p></div>\r\n          </div>\r\n          <div class=\"step\">\r\n            <span class=\"n\">5</span>\r\n            <div><h4>Other copies withdrawn</h4><p>&rsquo;Uthman then ordered that other partial or variant personal copies in circulation be gathered in and destroyed, removing the material basis for further dispute. Hafsa's original was returned to her.</p></div>\r\n          </div>\r\n        </div>\r\n\r\n        <div class=\"compare\">\r\n          <div>\r\n            <h4>Abu Bakr's compilation &middot; 12 AH</h4>\r\n            <ul>\r\n              <li><strong>Problem it solved:</strong> risk of losing verses as huffaz died in battle.</li>\r\n              <li><strong>Result:</strong> one single master copy, for safekeeping.</li>\r\n              <li><strong>Character:</strong> defensive &mdash; preserving what already existed.</li>\r\n            </ul>\r\n          </div>\r\n          <div>\r\n            <h4>&rsquo;Uthman's compilation &middot; c. 25 AH</h4>\r\n            <ul>\r\n              <li><strong>Problem it solved:</strong> risk of the empire splitting into rival regional recitations.</li>\r\n              <li><strong>Result:</strong> several identical copies, distributed and enforced as standard.</li>\r\n              <li><strong>Character:</strong> unifying &mdash; standardising for a growing, diverse ummah.</li>\r\n            </ul>\r\n          </div>\r\n        </div>\r\n\r\n        <div class=\"box box-elaborate\">\r\n          <p class=\"box-label\">&#9633; Elaborate &mdash; test your prediction</p>\r\n          <p>Go back to the guess you wrote at the start of the Preservation Before Compilation lesson. The real distinction examiners reward is not &ldquo;the first attempt was flawed&rdquo; &mdash; both compilations used the same rigorous method and produced the same accurate text. The distinction is <em>purpose</em>: Abu Bakr's was about preventing loss; &rsquo;Uthman's was about preventing division. State that distinction in your own sentence before moving on &mdash; this is one of the most commonly tested AO2 ideas in this whole topic.</p>\r\n        </div>\r\n\r\n        <h3 style=\"max-width:66ch; margin:40px auto 4px;\" class=\"prose-col\">Why this matters &mdash; the AO2 layer</h3>\r\n        <p class=\"prose-col\" style=\"margin-top:14px;\">For Muslims, the human effort behind both compilations is not separate from divine protection &mdash; it is understood as the very mechanism through which God's own promise was fulfilled: <em>&ldquo;Indeed, it is We who sent down the Qur&rsquo;an, and indeed, We will be its guardian&rdquo;</em> (Qur&rsquo;an 15:9). &rsquo;Uthman's standard text, the <strong>Uthmanic mus-haf</strong>, is the direct ancestor of every printed Qur&rsquo;an in circulation today, in every country, in every language edition &mdash; which is why a reciter in Jakarta and a reciter in Lagos are reading, letter for letter, the same verses. It is also why the companion &rsquo;Ali ibn Abi Talib, who might otherwise have contested &rsquo;Uthman's authority to order the destruction of other copies, is recorded defending the decision fully: &ldquo;Do not speak ill of &rsquo;Uthman&hellip; he did not do what he did with the mus-hafs except with our full agreement.&rdquo;</p>\r\n\r\n        <div class=\"examlab\">\r\n          <div class=\"examlab-head\">\r\n            <div>\r\n              <div class=\"tag\">PAST PAPER &middot; 0493/12 &middot; PAPER 1, Q2</div>\r\n              <h4>(a) Describe the roles of Abu Bakr, &rsquo;Umar and &rsquo;Uthman in compiling and preserving the Qur&rsquo;an. <span class=\"marks\">10 marks</span></h4>\r\n            </div>\r\n          </div>\r\n          <div class=\"examlab-body\">\r\n            <div class=\"ao-block\">\r\n              <p class=\"ao-label\">AO1 &middot; model answer points</p>\r\n              <ol>\r\n                <li>Abu Bakr: authorised the first compilation after &rsquo;Umar's warning following the losses at the Battle of Yamama; appointed Zayd ibn Thabit; kept the finished mus-haf in his own safekeeping.</li>\r\n                <li>&rsquo;Umar: the one who first proposed compilation to Abu Bakr; inherited the mus-haf as Caliph; passed it to his daughter Hafsa for safekeeping before his death.</li>\r\n                <li>&rsquo;Uthman: responded to Hudhayfah's warning about regional recitation disputes; borrowed Hafsa's mus-haf as the base text; commissioned Zayd's second committee; enforced one standard Quraysh-dialect text across the empire; withdrew variant copies.</li>\r\n                <li>Zayd ibn Thabit (credit him even though not named in the question &mdash; he is the thread connecting all three Caliphs): led both compilation efforts personally, verifying every verse against written and oral testimony each time.</li>\r\n              </ol>\r\n            </div>\r\n            <div class=\"ao-block\">\r\n              <p class=\"ao-label\">Part (b) &middot; AO2 &mdash; 4 marks</p>\r\n              <p style=\"color:var(--ink-soft);\">Typical prompt: <em>&ldquo;Significance of the Qur&rsquo;an being written as a book&rdquo;</em> or <em>&ldquo;Benefits of Muslims having a written Qur&rsquo;an.&rdquo;</em> Strong answers name concrete benefits: a fixed reference for settling disputes over wording; identical recitation across every Muslim country regardless of language; protection against the gradual drift or embellishment that affected earlier scriptures transmitted only orally or by hand across generations; a stable base text later scholars could safely build tafsir (interpretation) and Islamic law upon.</p>\r\n            </div>\r\n            <div class=\"examiner-note\"><strong>Examiner pattern:</strong> candidates frequently conflate the two compilations into one event, or credit &rsquo;Uthman with the huffaz-related trigger that actually belongs to Abu Bakr's. Keep the two timelines and two triggers distinct &mdash; the compare table above is designed to fix that specific confusion.</div>\r\n          </div>\r\n        </div>\r\n\r\n        <div style=\"max-width:66ch; margin:40px auto 0;\" class=\"prose-col\">\r\n          <h3 style=\"display:flex; align-items:baseline; gap:12px; flex-wrap:wrap;\">Ready to practise?</h3>\r\n          <p style=\"margin-top:10px; color:var(--ink-soft);\">A short Practice Quiz first, then a graded Quiz once you're confident. After that, take Unit 2.2's Unit Test.</p>\r\n        </div>\r\n\r\n        <div class=\"lesson-pager\">\r\n          <a class=\"pager-link\" href=\"../compilation-under-abu-bakr/quiz.html\"><span class=\"pg-label\">&larr; Back</span><div class=\"pg-title\">Compilation under Abu Bakr Quiz</div></a>\r\n          <a class=\"pager-link next\" href=\"practice.html\"><span class=\"pg-label\">Next &rarr;</span><div class=\"pg-title\">Practice Quiz</div></a>\r\n        </div>\r\n      </div>\r\n    </section>";
